Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Heavy Water Scraper
- Story & Context: DJ Vessel found the initial magnetic tape fragment sticking to the rust-crated lining of the Avon capsule, its iron oxide peeling away like damp bark. He locked himself in a Redcliffe basement for three days, looping a five-second stretch of degraded 1994 fiddle noise over a high-speed Amen break until the track took on its own frantic life. When Maeve O'Connell added her live, weeping bow work over the top, the local dockworkers outside mistook the bass vibration for an engine test on an outbound freighter. She sang into the ribbon mic between takes, murmuring, "pull the signal down before the river takes the frame."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric single art showing a rusted iron lock-gate jammed open by drifting dark timber, surrounded by churning, foam-flecked black water under a moody, violet twilight sky with handwritten tape-label typography overlaid across the image.

Track 2: Skerry-Slang
- Story & Context: Named after the local dockworker dialect for hidden rocks beneath the harbor line, this track captures the tension of an illegal dawn rave held inside an abandoned granary in early 2025. Maeve improvised the central melody while watching police cruisers circle the opposite bank of the river, their blue sirens reflecting off the stagnant dock water. The track shifts dynamically from an isolated, aching fiddle breakdown into an earth-rattling liquid funk sub-drop just as the sun breaks through the grey mist. Above the sub-bass rumble, a sampled vocal loop continuously whispers, "we speak in rocks and iron so they never hear us drown."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Stark visual scene of a rain-streaked warehouse window overlooking a fog-shrouded harbor, illuminated by the cold blue light of a distant lighthouse beam cutting through the damp darkness.

Track 3: The Ghost of Dock 4
- Story & Context: The closing track was recorded in one continuous live take using the original corrupted cassette tape as a master delay loop, creating a haunting tail that sounds like a ship's horn echoing off stone walls. Legend in the Bristol scene holds that the master file was almost lost when a storm flooded the studio basement, submerging the mixing desk in eight inches of muddy brackish water. They dried the tape reel with a hair dryer and pressed the test vinyl that very evening, preserving the soft, water-warbled crackle audible under the final fiddle solo. The track ends on a dying vocal chant, fragile and lone: "leave the reel spinning when the high tide comes."
